BT /F1 10 Tf 30 800 Td 24 TL (Sorting exam. Section I: ten single-choice questions.) Tj T* (1. Which statement about quicksort partition is true?) Tj T* (A. All items become sorted. B. Items are grouped relative to a pivot.) Tj T* (C. Every split is equal. D. The pivot must be the smallest item.) Tj T* (2. Why is standard merge sort stable?) Tj T* (A. It never compares keys. B. It uses a heap.) Tj T* (C. Ties can be taken from the left half first. D. It uses random pivots.) Tj T* (3. What does removing the maximum from a max-heap require?) Tj T* (A. Remove any leaf. B. Reverse the whole heap.) Tj T* (C. Sort all leaves. D. Replace the root by the last item and sift down.) Tj T* (4. What does one complete left-to-right bubble-sort pass ensure?) Tj T* (A. The smallest is last. B. Everything is sorted.) Tj T* (C. The largest unsorted item reaches the right end. D. No swaps occur.) Tj T* (5. Which input usually favors insertion sort?) Tj T* (A. Nearly sorted input. B. Only reversed input.) Tj T* (C. Only random input. D. Only all-distinct keys.) Tj T* (6. What does selection sort do at each position?) Tj T* (A. Pick a random pivot. B. Merge two halves.) Tj T* (C. Build a frequency array. D. Select the minimum remaining item.) Tj T* (7. What makes counting sort useful?) Tj T* (A. Arbitrary real keys. B. A small bounded integer key range.) Tj T* (C. No access to keys. D. Only linked lists.) Tj T* (8. What does LSD radix sort need from each digit-sorting pass?) Tj T* (A. Instability. B. Randomness. C. Stability. D. Recursion.) Tj T* (9. What does stability mean in sorting records by key?) Tj T* (A. Equal-key records keep their relative order. B. Values never move.) Tj T* (C. Keys cannot repeat. D. The algorithm uses constant memory.) Tj T* (10. What does an in-place sorting algorithm primarily minimize?) Tj T* (A. Input size. B. Auxiliary storage. C. Key values. D. Number of duplicates.) Tj T* (Section II: five fill-in-the-blank questions.) Tj ET
